{"records":[{"id":"jzd29z82fk","title":"MiR-182, miR-371a-5p and miR-331-3p are associated with pulmonary and extrapulmonary tuberculosis and linked to cholesterol metabolism-related gene networks","description":"This dataset contains the derived bioinformatic tables used in the study of miR-182, miR-371a-5p, and miR-331-3p in pulmonary and extrapulmonary tuberculosis. It includes DEG outputs from GSE83456 for PTB versus healthy controls and EPTB versus healthy controls, a cholesterol metabolism gene list, predicted target-gene lists for the three selected miRNAs, and an integrated sheet used for overlap and candidate-gene prioritisation. Original transcriptomic data are publicly available from GEO under accession GSE83456.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-17T13:34:27.084Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jzd29z82fk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jzd29z82fk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mirza Ameer Faizan ","last_name":"Ali","orcid":"0000-0002-3230-3984"},{"first_name":"Zawar ","last_name":"Hussain","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Afia Muhammad ","last_name":"Akram","orcid":"0000-0003-2610-2083"},{"first_name":"Syed Zeeshan ","last_name":"Haider","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mariya ","last_name":"Azam","orcid":"0000-0002-7214-8536"},{"first_name":"Jude","last_name":"Alao","orcid":"0000-0002-8575-2070"},{"first_name":"Zunaira ","last_name":"Javaid","orcid":"0009-0004-3202-2922"},{"first_name":"Bushra ","last_name":"Iftikhar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aqsa ","last_name":"Ikram","orcid":"0000-0001-7292-6134"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bioinformatics","Gene Regulatory Network","microRNA","Pulmonary Tuberculosis","Tuberculosis","Extra Pulmonary Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"m8s629jyg3","title":"Role of Dynamin 2 in mitochondrial fission and cell cycle regulation in pulmonary arterial hypertension: Dysregulation of a miR-124-3p-STAT3-DNM2-DRP1-RGCC pathway links fission and cell proliferation","description":"Background: Mitochondrial fission is mediated by Dynamin-related protein 1 (DRP1). Excessive mitochondrial fission supports rapid cell cycle progression in hyperproliferative cells by synchronizing nuclear and mitochondrial division through a process called mitotic fission. However, DRP1 alone appears insufficient to complete the terminal phase of fission and the mechanism linking fission to cell cycle progression remain unclear. In this study, we propose that Dynamin 2 (DNM2) interacts with DRP1 to regulate mitochondrial fission and cell cycle progression.  Here we show that DNM2 is upregulated in pulmonary arterial smooth muscle cells in human and rodent pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH PASMC), contributing to disease pathophysiology.\n\nMethods: Mitochondrial morphology, protein colocalization and fission were evaluated STED microscopy, protein interactions were examined by immunoprecipitation and transcriptomic changes by RNA-seq. DNM2 expression was measured in PASMC and lungs from PAH patients and rat models of PH-induced by monocrotaline (MCT) or SU5416/hypoxia (Su/Hx). The effects of silencing DNM2 on cell proliferation, cell cycle progression and apoptosis were measured by flow cytometry. Publicly available datasets were analyzed for single-cell RNA-Seq. In vivo siRNA against DNM2 was nebulized to MCT- and Su/Hx-PH rats and therapeutic effects were evaluated by cardiac catheterization and histological analyses.\n\nResults: DNM2 expression is increased PAH PASMC. Mechanistically, DNM2 interacts with DRP1 through its GTPase domain, facilitating Drp1 recruitment to mitochondria and promoting fission. Silencing DNM2 supresses fission and cell proliferation and promotes apoptosis. Additionally, siDNM2 induces G1/G0 cell cycle arrest by decreasing the expression of Regulator of Cell Cycle (RGCC), leading to downstream modulation of CDK4, Cyclin D1 and p27kip1. In contrast, augmenting DNM2 in normal PASMC increases fission and drives proliferation. The elevated DNM2 observed in PAH is mediated by reduced miR-124-3p levels and STAT3 activation. Together these findings suggest a miR-124-3p-STAT3-DNM2-DRP1-RGCC axis that promotes mitotic fission is upregulated in multiple cell types in PAH lungs including PASMC, airway epithelium, endothelial cells, fibroblasts, and macrophages. Nebulized siDNM2 effectively regresses established PAH in rat models of both sexes. \n\nConclusion: DNM2 functions as a mediator in the terminal steps of DRP1-dependent mitochondrial fission and represents a promising therapeutic target in PAH. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-02T19:01:32.127Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/m8s629jyg3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/m8s629jyg3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Charles","last_name":"Hindmarch","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Asish","last_name":"Das Gupta","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kuang-Hueih","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Danchen","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Siddartha","last_name":"Yerramilli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Patricia","last_name":"Lima","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ashley","last_name":"Martin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rachel","last_name":"Bentley","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Benjamin","last_name":"Ott","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tanvi","last_name":"Nandani","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jeff","last_name":"Mewburn","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lian","last_name":"Tian","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ruaa","last_name":"Al-Qazazi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Isaac","last_name":"Emon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pierce","last_name":"Colpman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lindsay","last_name":"Jefferson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Curtis","last_name":"Noordhof","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Oliver ","last_name":"Jones","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stephen","last_name":"Archer","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/00qbpyp73","name":"Heart and Stroke Foundation","grant_id":null},{"id":null,"name":"William J Henderson Foundation","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Mitochondrial RNA","RNA Sequencing","Transcriptomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"g3xxjdhwkt","title":"UBE2M is a stress-inducible dual E2 for neddylation and ubiquitylation that promotes targeted degradation of UBE2F. Weihua Zhou et al.","description":"UBE2M and UBE2F are two family members of neddylation E2 conjugating enzyme that, together with E3s, activate CRLs (Cullin-RING Ligases) by catalyzing cullin neddylation. However, whether and how two E2s cross-talk with each other are largely unknown. Here, we report that UBE2M is a stress-inducible gene subjected to cis-transactivation by HIF-1α and AP1, and MLN4924, a small molecule inhibitor of E1 NEDD8-activating enzyme (NAE), up-regulates UBE2M via blocking degradation of HIF-1α and AP1. UBE2M is a dual E2 for targeted ubiquitylation and degradation of UBE2F, acting as a neddylation E2 to activate CUL3-Keap1 E3 under physiological condition, but as an ubiquitylation E2 for Parkin-DJ-1 E3 under stressed conditions. UBE2M-induced UBE2F degradation leads to CRL5 inactivation and subsequent NOXA accumulation to suppress the growth of lung cancer cells.  Collectively, our study establishes a negative regulatory axis between two neddylation E2s with UBE2M ubiquitylating UBE2F, and two CRLs with CRL3 inactivating CRL5.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-06-06T18:11:47.193Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/g3xxjdhwkt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/g3xxjdhwkt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yi","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Weihua","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jie","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mingjia","last_name":"Tan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jinfang","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Haomin","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hua","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wenyi","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Ubiquitylation","Lung Carcinoma"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"53wrc86y6s","title":"Therapeutic application of extracellular vesicular EGFR isoform D as a co-drug to target squamous cell cancers with tyrosine kinase inhibitors","description":"Supplementary Information for manuscript \"Therapeutic application of extracellular vesicular EGFR isoform D as a co-drug to target squamous cell cancers with tyrosine kinase inhibitors\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-07-03T00:11:09.81Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/53wrc86y6s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/53wrc86y6s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Darren","last_name":"Toh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hui sun","last_name":"Leong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fui Teen","last_name":"Chong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dorival Mendes - Junior","last_name":"Rodrigues","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Meng Jie","last_name":"Ren","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xue Lin ","last_name":"Kwang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dawn P X","last_name":"Lau","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Po-Hsien","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andre Luiz","last_name":"Vettore","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bin Tean","last_name":"Teh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel S W","last_name":"Tan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"N Gopalakrishna","last_name":"Iyer","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Receptor Trafficking","Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor","Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitor","Head and Neck Cancer","Extracellular Vesicle"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j7bz9v33w8","title":"MicroRNAs as a therapeutic target in IgA nephropathy in Indian population","description":"Immunoglobulin A Nephropathy (IgAN) is the most occurring glomerular disease with rapid development to End Stage Renal Disease requiring renal replacement therapy. Genome-wide studies suggest geographical variations in genetic susceptibility to IgAN and disease progression have been shown to play a vital role in physio pathological state. In this study, we have identified new microRNAs allied with IgAN in Indian population. The miRNA was isolated from kidney biopsies of IgAN patients and healthy control (healthy tissue from patients with renal cell carcinoma). Small RNA library preparation was performed, followed by Next Generation Sequencing on the ION S5 Torrent at 1x50 base pairs. Later the miRNA expression analyses were carried and obtained high-quality reads from BAM file. The sequence of miRNA reads was mapped to reference Human genome (hg38). The Partek Flow Genomic Analysis Software was used to compare the miRNAs expression between healthy participants and patients with IgAN. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-09T06:57:03.13Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j7bz9v33w8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j7bz9v33w8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"POORNACHANDRA","last_name":"Yedla","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anindita","last_name":"Tripathy","orcid":null},{"first_name":"SaiRam Reddy","last_name":"Keithi ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ravikanth","last_name":"Vishnubhotla","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Kidney Disorder","Clinical Nephrology","Immunoglobulin A"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"kzkph6c2hg","title":"OSI-027 and MRC-5 cells","description":"Hyperoxia promotes time-dependent cell proliferation and the differentiation of lung fibroblasts, as well as the activation of the mTOR signaling pathway. The dual mTORC1/2 inhibitor OSI-027 inhibits the proliferation and differentiation of MRC-5 cells under hyperoxia exposure and is expected to provide a new target for clinical intervention in hyperoxic pulmonary fibrosis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-08-22T14:55:29.511Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kzkph6c2hg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kzkph6c2hg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hongxing","last_name":"Dang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mammalian Target of Rapamycin","Cell Proliferation","Pulmonary Fibrosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4t45fc835x","title":"Network Pharmacology-Based Identification of Pharmacological Mechanisms of Inula japonica Thunb. on Non-small Cell Lung Cancer","description":"This was the data underlying the study, which included as follows:\nTable S1 Putative targets in IJT\nTable S2  Known therapeutic NSCLC-related targets\nTable S3  Known therapeutic targets of IJT acting on NSCLC\nTable S4 Topological features of all nodes in the compound-compound target network\nTable S5 Protein-protein interactions of therapeutic targets of IJT on NSCLC\nTable S6 Topological features of all nodes in the T-T network of  putative IJT targets, known therapeutic target and interactional human proteins\nTable S7 Topological features of hub nodes in theT-T network for IJT against NSCLC extracted from Fig. 4\nTable S8 Significant pathways\nTable S9 Molecular docking","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-09T08:53:18.146Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4t45fc835x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4t45fc835x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Huiqin","last_name":"Qian","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Supplementation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wtst3x3kbc","title":"High Dimensional Immune Profiling Reveals CD39 as a Correlate of Tuberculosis Disease Severity - Data and Analysis","description":"This dataset contains the raw data, processed data, and code required the replicate Watt et al., 2026 (doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.07.01.735885).\n\nAbstract: \nImmune biomarkers of tuberculosis (TB) disease severity present a challenging area of research that remains poorly understood. New technologies are able to perform larger, unbiased studies that can unravel the complex host-pathogen dynamics occurring during a Mycobacterium tuberculosis infection, the causative agent of TB. In this study, we designed a high dimensional approach combining viable bacterial burden (CFU, colony forming units) with time-of-flight mass cytometry (CyTOF) analysis to profile differences in cell-type abundance and cell-type specific protein expression during states of low, intermediate and high TB disease burden. Broadly, we segregated cell-type specific immune responses into those driven by bacterial burden and/or the mycobacterial infection strain. Interrogating these immune signatures allowed us to identify ATP-catabolizing protein CD39 as a correlate of disease severity. Treatment of mice with a small molecule inhibitor of CD39 promoted effector T cell functions and CD4 T cell expansion during Mtb infection. Collectively, our data defines the differential lung immune environment between various mycobacterial disease severity states and uncovers a potential immune biomarker of infection and therapeutic immunomodulating target to aid in the treatment of TB. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-25T09:27:40.889Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wtst3x3kbc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wtst3x3kbc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jacqueline","last_name":"Watt","orcid":"0009-0009-8991-0679"},{"first_name":"Dustin","last_name":"Sokolowski","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jun","last_name":"Liu","orcid":"0000-0002-5815-2483"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":"PJT-156261 and PJT-186285"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h531d29","name":"Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council of Canada","grant_id":"PGS-D and PDF"}],"categories":["Infectious Disease","Mouse Model","Tuberculosis","Mycobacterium","Mass Cytometry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"p47zrwrxct","title":"Disrupting BRD4-BD1–p62 Engagement Restores Protective Autophagy","description":"Protective autophagy is profoundly impaired in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF), yet no therapies directly restore this fundamental cellular defense mechanism. Here, we identify the BRD4-BD1-p62 interface as a druggable regulator of protective autophagy and develop SKLB-39b, a BRD4-BD1-selective inhibitor that disrupts this interaction. By engaging Leu92 and Ile146, SKLB-39b restores ULK1-dependent autophagy, suppresses fibroblast activation and epithelial-mesenchymal transition, and attenuates pulmonary fibrosis with improved tolerability relative to pan-BET inhibition in the mouse studies performed here. These findings establish a mechanistic framework for domain-selective BET inhibition and demonstrate how disrupting a disease-associated protein-protein interaction within an epigenetic reader can restore protective autophagy. More broadly, this work identifies the BRD4-BD1-p62 interface as a therapeutic opportunity for autophagy-deficient fibrotic diseases.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-19T09:10:26.098Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/p47zrwrxct","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/p47zrwrxct"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jifa","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yu","last_name":"Cao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chengyong","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yang","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Juncheng","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Doudou","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yao","last_name":"Xiao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Binghan","last_name":"Xue","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Cailing","last_name":"Gan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuxi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tinghong","last_name":"Ye","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Liang","last_name":"Ouyang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Screening"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2c97jkvx9c","title":"UFMylation maintains YAP stability to promote vascular endothelial cell senescence ","description":"Endothelial cell senescence is an accomplice for vascular aging, which leads to cardiovascular diseases (CVD). Evidences showed that Hippo- Yes-associated protein (YAP) signaling pathway plays an essential role in aging-associated CVD. However, the exact role of YAP protein in endothelial cell senescence remains not fully clear. Here, we reported that YAP was elevated in senescent human umbilical vein endothelial cells (HUVECs) and inhibition of YAP by either specific siRNAs or inhibitor Verteporfin could attenuate HUVECs senescence. In contrast, overexpression of YAP induces cell senescence. Besides, we found that UFMylation activity and YAP were both elevated in senescent cells. Mechanistically, we found that UFMylation, a newly identified ubiquitin-like modification with essential biological functions, maintains the stability of YAP and further found that YAP is a substrate for UFMylation. Importantly, we found that compound 8.5, an inhibitor of E1 of UFMylation, can attenuate cell senescence with reduced YAP protein and alleviate vascular aging and improve cardiac function in aged mice. Therefore, compound 8.5 may be a potential small molecule for delaying vascular aging. Together, our finding provides molecular mechanism by which UFMylation maintains YAP stability and exerts an important role in promoting cell senescence, and identified that a previously unrecognized UFMylation is a potential therapeutic target for anti-aging. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-29T07:58:34.298Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2c97jkvx9c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2c97jkvx9c"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"yanan","last_name":"liu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Aging","Vascular Injury","Vascular Endothelium"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":143334,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":137,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":143334,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":142544,"restricted":70,"embargoed":720},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":3517},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2302},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":692},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":471},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":440},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":433},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":419},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":399},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":339},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":275}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":140680},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1911},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":186},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":135},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":79},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":58},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":40},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":2998},{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":2328},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":526},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":408},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":368}]}}